  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM EDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nEarly morning water vapor and RAP analysis shows midlevel troughing \nover the Great Lakes, extending southward from a deeper trough \nmoving east of Hudson Bay. An embedded shortwave is apparent over \nthe Dakotas, but this looks to drop well south of us later this \nmorning. Rather a dry airmass is allowing for mostly clear skies, \nand with winds falling back somewhat quicker than guidance \npreviously suggested, some patchy fog is not totally out of the \nquestion over the next few hours across the interior UP. Otherwise, \ntemperatures currently hover in the lower to mid 50s over the \nwestern half of the UP and the upper 50s to the east; expect lows to \nrange in the mid 40s (west) to lower 50s (east). \n\nToday, high pressure expanding over the Plains extends ridging into \nthe Great Lakes, with a quiet and sunny day ahead as highs peak in \nthe 70s away from Superior, and in the mid/upper 60s lakeside. \nTemperatures fall back into the 40s to lower 50s under clear skies \ntonight, then climb higher again Friday as the ridge axis slides \neastward and we become situated over increasing SW flow ahead of our \nnext approaching trough (more on that shortly). Expect highs in the \nlower 80s for most Friday afternoon. \n\nMeanwhile, seasonably strong 500 mb zonal flow evolves Fri/Sat over \nthe northern tier of the western half of the CONUS. A lead shortwave \ntrough ejecting across the Upper Midwest will foster a compact \nsurface low/front over the northern Plains, near which convection is \nexpected to erupt Friday afternoon/evening within a plume of \nmoderate instability. The front sweeps across the UP Friday night \nwith rain and thunderstorms expected for the west half, weakening \nwith eastward extent along with the instability. Severe weather is \nnot expected at this time due to the waning instability. It will be \nmore seasonable and dry behind the front on Saturday and likely into \nSunday. \n\nLooking ahead into early next week, there are indications that a \nwetter pattern evolves as a Colorado low develops in response to \ndeep troughing over the northern Rockies. Good poleward Gulf \nmoisture advection is modeled (mean PWATs in the 1.5-1.75 inch range \nlocally per the LREF, which is toward the 95 pctl of climo), which \nwill be lifted atop a low-level frontal boundary to provide a \npotentially long-lived period of rainfall Monday into Tuesday in the \nUpper Midwest. Cluster analysis shows the main mode of variability \nis tied to the overall amplification of the encroaching trough and \ndownstream ridge, with a more (less) amplified height field favoring \na wetter (drier) solution. About a quarter of the LREF members are \nslower with the trough, which would favor another dry day on Monday, \nas well. Regardless, LREF indicates a 50-60% chance of accumulating \n1\" of rainfall by Tuesday evening, and a 20-30% chance of 2\". Medium \nrange AI-based convective hazards forecasts indicate any severe \nweather risk remains to our south during this period.",
